# Hooks

A custom hook shares stateful logic. Each call has its own state. To share the state itself, lift it into context.

The name starts with `use` and a capital letter. A function that calls no hook is a plain function, such as `mapProductCard`.

One hook per file. `useProductCards` lives in `use-product-cards.ts`. The name is the use case: what it takes and what it returns.

Extract a hook when it composes several queries or mutations, when the same stateful logic is copied, or when an Effect synchronizes with something outside React. Do not wrap a single `useState`. Do not wrap one query and one mapper. Do not add a lifecycle wrapper such as `useMount`, `useEffectOnce`, or `useUpdateEffect`.

The body runs on every render, so it stays pure. Call it unconditionally, and pass reactive values in as arguments. An event handler that an Effect calls is wrapped in `useEffectEvent`, so a new function does not re-subscribe the Effect.

Mapping stays in a mapper from `@/mappers`. The component calls that mapper. A hook that already exists for several queries calls the mapper too. Wrap the call in `useMemo` only when the memoization rule says so. UI state stays in the component.

```tsx
// Bad: a hook that only forwards one query
export function useCampaignBoard() {
  const query = useCampaigns();
  const campaigns = (query.data ?? []).map(mapCampaignRow);

  return { campaigns, isLoading: query.isLoading };
}

// Good: the board calls the query and the mapper
const query = useCampaigns();
const campaigns = (query.data ?? []).map(mapCampaignRow);
```
